#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""glaeda-fleet-cas-prune: keep a cmux mini's fleet compilation-cache stores within budget.

    glaeda-fleet-cas-prune                 plan: sizes, budgets, what would go
    glaeda-fleet-cas-prune --apply         prune (the hourly LaunchAgent from glaeda-mini-setup)
    glaeda-fleet-cas-prune --apply --store-gc-only
                                           only the fleet-store gc: the store host's own hourly agent
                                           (glaeda-fleet-cas store with a custom store root, such as
                                           the coordinator's external SSD), which has no mini caches
    glaeda-fleet-cas-prune --apply --node-only
                                           only the node-store cap (glaeda-disk's pressure passes, which run
                                           before every job and under the runner floor)
    glaeda-fleet-cas-prune --apply --cap-only --node-gib N --local-cas-gib N
                                           only the two caps: the node store under N and a reader's local
                                           CAS whole once over N, under the local CAS's own in-use rules; no
                                           sweep, no fleet-store gc (glaeda-disk's pressure pass while the
                                           volume's build caches are over their budget). When ps fails the
                                           node cap still holds and the local CAS waits for the next pass.

Two stores under /Users/Shared/cmux-build-fleet/xcode (docs/CMUX_BUILD_SLOT_MODES.md):

  cas         Xcode's own local CAS. The node does not manage it and its layout is Xcode's, so
              it only ever goes whole, once it is over --local-cas-gib (20): its standalone
              files (v8.<offset>.leaf) are named from v8.index, so removing one alone would leave
              the index naming an object that is gone. It goes while nothing has it open (no
              process holds its `lock` file, which LLVM's on-disk CAS holds shared while open)
              and the host lock is free, and either no build runs, or it is idle: nothing in it
              was written or read in the last --local-cas-quiet-min minutes (60), nor since the
              oldest running build started, so no running build has used it. (hq#11385: cmux9s
              always runs unrelated runner jobs, so "no build runs" never held there and its CAS
              sat at 29 GiB over the 20 GiB cap, under the disk floor.) On a reader it only
              speeds up repeat builds (everything in it comes back from the node); the writer
              empties it at the start of every writer build, so the writer's is left alone.
  node-store  the node daemon's cache of the fleet store: one file per object (cas/) or index
              entry (kv/), named by hash in two-hex-digit shards. Any file may go: a missing one
              is fetched again, and its signature re-verified, on the next lookup. Oldest mtime
              goes first until the store is under 90% of --node-gib. stats.json stays. It is a
              hard cap: it holds while builds run (on a busy mini a build always runs, and
              cmux15's writer store reached 73 GiB, 2026-10-26). Removing a file mid-build is a
              cache miss, never a wrong result: a missing object is fetched again from the fleet
              store and its ID recomputed from the bytes (get_or_fetch), or the build compiles; a
              reader's index entries are signed by the writer and fetched again the same way.
              On the writer only cas/ objects go, which are content-addressed and written through
              before they are acknowledged. Its kv/ index entries stay: the fleet store keeps the
              first value for a key, so a writer that lost its own entry and computed another
              would then serve a value the fleet store does not hold (kv_put).

A missing local CAS in the middle of a build that uses it breaks the build, so the local CAS goes
only while no build can be using it: none of its lock files is held, and either no xcodebuild, Xcode
build service or GitHub Actions Runner.Worker runs, or its newest mtime or atime is older than both
the oldest such process's start and the quiet window. Sizes and the plan are worked out without any
lock; the local CAS rename runs under the fleet host lock (host.lock, the same flock with-host-lock
and the runner hook take) and the CAS's own lock files, all taken without waiting, after checking
once more. Holding the host lock refuses new jobs and takes idle listeners offline (#1206), so it is held only for
the rename; the renamed local CAS is removed after the lock is released.

The fleet store (xcode/fleet-store, on the store host only) is pruned by `fleet-cas gc`, which
knows which entries are still read (glaeda#1216): kept are entries used within --gc-keep-days,
everything they reach, and anything written in the last day. It runs at most once a day, from
this same hourly job, and always as a dry run first: the real run goes ahead only when the dry
run reports 0 "kept entries naming no stored object" (the check of gc's object-ID heuristic),
otherwise the numbers are logged and nothing is deleted. gc takes no lock: a race with the running
store only turns into a cache miss, never a wrong answer (gc.rs), so it is not gated on builds. While the store's volume has under 15% free, gc keeps entries
used within half of --gc-keep-days (at least 3) and may run every 6 hours instead of daily.
Prints one JSON line per run.

Disk errors: a walk (sizes, the node cap, last-use times, gc's own) that meets an entry it cannot stat, list or
unlink (EIO from a failing drive, ESTALE, EACCES) skips it, counts it and reports it, never ends the pass: the line
gains `unreadable_entries`, and every run that walked a store replaces DISK_ERRORS_DIR/<mode>.json
(glaeda-disk-errors/v1: fleet-cas-store-gc.json for --store-gc-only, else fleet-cas-prune.json), which NOW reads as a
disk-error row (leo-workspace tools/now_host_services.py, the registry's `disk_errors`). A file that vanished
mid-walk (ENOENT) is a race with the store, not a disk error, and is not counted. cmuxterm-hq
docs/handbook/one-read-takes-down-the-pass.md.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import datetime
import errno
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import fcntl
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path(os.environ.get("GLAEDA_FLEET_CAS_ROOT", "/Users/Shared/cmux-build-fleet/xcode"))
HOST_LOCK = Path(os.environ.get("FLEET_HOST_LOCK", "/Users/Shared/cmux-build-fleet/host.lock"))
GIB = 1024**3
# A build that can read the node: any xcodebuild, or a CI job (any runner dir, versioned bin too).
# Xcode's build service can outlive xcodebuild with the CAS plugin still open.
BUSY = re.compile(r"(^|/)(xcodebuild|SWBBuildService|XCBBuildService)(\s|$)"
                  r"|/actions-runner[^/\s]*/bin(?:\.[0-9.]+)?/Runner\.Worker(?:\s|$)")
PRUNING = ".cas.pruning-"  # a local CAS renamed aside for deletion
# Standalone objects of the local CAS: written once, named from its index, many (96k on cmux9s), so the re-check
# under the locks skips them (writing one also changes its directory's mtime, which is checked).
CAS_STANDALONE = re.compile(r"\.\d+\.(?:leaf|leaf\+0|data)$")
GC_EVERY = 86400  # seconds between fleet-store gc runs
GC_EVERY_PRESSED = 6 * 3600  # ... while the store's volume is under GC_PRESSURE free
GC_PRESSURE = 0.15
GC_MIN_KEEP_DAYS = 3
GC_REPORT = re.compile(r"kept entries naming no stored object: (\d+)")
# Crash reports of the processes that load Xcode's CAS plugin (libToolchainCASPlugin.dylib).
CRASH_REPORTS = Path(os.environ.get("GLAEDA_FLEET_CAS_CRASH_REPORTS",
                                    os.path.expanduser("~/Library/Logs/DiagnosticReports")))
CAS_CLIENTS = ("SWBBuildService", "XCBBuildService", "swift-frontend", "clang")
CAS_PLUGIN = "libToolchainCASPlugin.dylib"
CRASH_LOOKBACK = 7 * 86400  # crash reports older than this are not read
DISK_ERRORS_DIR = Path(os.environ.get("GLAEDA_DISK_ERRORS_DIR")
                       or os.path.expanduser("~/.local/state/glaeda/disk-errors"))
DISK_ERRORS_SCHEMA = "glaeda-disk-errors/v1"
DISK_ERRORS_EXAMPLES = 5
GC_UNREADABLE = re.compile(r"unreadable entries skipped: (\d+)")  # fleet-cas gc's report (gc.rs), when it has one
# This run's disk errors: entries a walk could not read (count, a few examples), and whether any store was walked
# (a deferred run read nothing, so it must not clear a count with a zero).
DISK_ERRORS: dict = {"count": 0, "examples": [], "walked": False}


def disk_error(error: OSError, path: str | os.PathLike | None = None) -> None:
    """Count one entry a walk could not read (os.walk's onerror, and each per-entry stat or unlink). ENOENT is the
    store racing the walk (a file removed between listing and stat), not a disk error."""
    if isinstance(error, FileNotFoundError):
        return
    DISK_ERRORS["count"] += 1
    if len(DISK_ERRORS["examples"]) < DISK_ERRORS_EXAMPLES:
        where = os.fspath(path) if path is not None else (error.filename or "?")
        DISK_ERRORS["examples"].append(f"{where}: {type(error).__name__} errno {error.errno}: {error.strerror}")


def walked(root: Path) -> None:
    DISK_ERRORS["walked"] = True


def emit(rec: dict, service: str) -> None:
    """Print the run's line, with its disk errors, and replace DISK_ERRORS_DIR/SERVICE.json when a store was walked."""
    if DISK_ERRORS["count"]:
        rec["unreadable_entries"] = DISK_ERRORS["count"]
        rec["unreadable_examples"] = DISK_ERRORS["examples"]
    print(json.dumps(rec))
    if not (rec.get("apply") and DISK_ERRORS["walked"]):
        return
    report = {"schema": DISK_ERRORS_SCHEMA, "service": service, "at": int(time.time()),
              "root": os.fspath(ROOT / "fleet-store" if service == "fleet-cas-store-gc" else ROOT),
              "unreadable": DISK_ERRORS["count"],
              "examples": DISK_ERRORS["examples"]}
    path = DISK_ERRORS_DIR / f"{service}.json"
    try:
        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        staged = path.with_name(f".{path.name}.{os.getpid()}")
        staged.write_text(json.dumps(report, sort_keys=True) + "\n")
        staged.replace(path)
    except OSError:
        pass  # a report that cannot be written never fails the run


def load_glaeda_disk(path: Path | None = None):
    """glaeda-disk installed beside this script, as a module (registered in sys.modules, which its dataclasses need)."""
    path = Path(__file__).with_name("glaeda-disk") if path is None else path
    name = "glaeda_disk_for_prune"
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(name, os.fspath(path))
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(name, loader)
    gd = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    sys.modules[name] = gd
    try:
        loader.exec_module(gd)
    except BaseException:
        sys.modules.pop(name, None)
        raise
    return gd


def reserved_above_floor(gd=None) -> str:
    """Why this host is left alone: a fleet reservation holds it (glaeda-disk's host_reservation, which fails closed)
    and free disk is at or above its runner floor. "" when not."""
    try:
        gd = load_glaeda_disk() if gd is None else gd
        why = gd.host_reservation()
        floor = gd.runner_floor_gib()
    except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001  (fail closed: no glaeda-disk to ask, so treat the host as reserved)
        return f"cannot read fleet reservations ({type(error).__name__}); kept as reserved"
    if not why:
        return ""
    free = shutil.disk_usage(ROOT if ROOT.is_dir() else Path.home()).free / GIB
    return "" if floor > 0 and free < floor else f"{why}; {free:.0f} GiB free, floor {floor:g}"


def toolchain_overlap(paths: list[Path], gd=None) -> str:
    """Why none of PATHS may be pruned: one is at, inside or above the job toolchain (glaeda-disk toolchain_held: an
    aliased node-store/cas or cas that leads into ~/.rustup or ~/.cargo/bin), or glaeda-disk cannot be asked (fail
    closed). "" when every path is clear."""
    try:
        gd = load_glaeda_disk() if gd is None else gd
        held = [os.fspath(p) for p in paths if gd.toolchain_held(p, fresh=True)]
    except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001
        return f"cannot check the job toolchain ({type(error).__name__}); nothing pruned"
    return f"{', '.join(held)} overlaps the job toolchain; nothing pruned" if held else ""


def busy(commands: list[str]) -> str:
    for c in commands:
        if BUSY.search(c):
            return c.strip()[:160]
    return ""


def parse_etime(text: str) -> int | None:
    """ps's etime, [[dd-]hh:]mm:ss, in seconds; None when it is not that."""
    days, _, rest = text.strip().rpartition("-")
    parts = rest.split(":")
    if not 2 <= len(parts) <= 3 or not all(p.isdigit() for p in parts) or (days and not days.isdigit()):
        return None
    secs = 0
    for p in parts:
        secs = secs * 60 + int(p)
    return secs + int(days or 0) * 86400


def build_starts(now: float | None = None) -> list[float] | None:
    """When each running build (a BUSY process) started, or None when ps fails or an age cannot be read."""
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(["ps", "-axww", "-o", "etime=,command="], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    if out.returncode != 0:
        return None
    starts = []
    for line in out.stdout.splitlines():
        etime, _, command = line.strip().partition(" ")
        if not BUSY.search(command.strip()):
            continue
        secs = parse_etime(etime)
        if secs is None:
            return None
        starts.append(now - secs)
    return starts


def last_touch(path: Path, quick: bool = False) -> float:
    """The newest file mtime or atime, or directory mtime, under path (a directory's atime moves when this very walk
    lists it, so it is not counted; a new object changes its directory's mtime). quick skips standalone objects."""
    newest = 0.0
    for dirpath, _, files in os.walk(path, onerror=disk_error):
        try:
            newest = max(newest, os.lstat(dirpath).st_mtime)
        except OSError as e:
            disk_error(e, dirpath)
        for name in files:
            if quick and CAS_STANDALONE.search(name):
                continue
            try:
                st = os.lstat(os.path.join(dirpath, name))
            except OSError as e:
                disk_error(e, os.path.join(dirpath, name))
                continue
            newest = max(newest, st.st_mtime, st.st_atime)
    return newest


def cas_locks(local: Path) -> list[int] | None:
    """Every `lock` file of the local CAS (cas/lock, cas/<name>/lock) taken exclusive without waiting, or None, with
    none kept, when one is held: LLVM's on-disk CAS holds its lock shared while a process has it open."""
    held: list[int] = []
    for dirpath, dirs, files in os.walk(local, onerror=disk_error):
        if dirpath != os.fspath(local):
            dirs[:] = []  # never down into the object directories
        if "lock" not in files:
            continue
        try:
            fd = os.open(os.path.join(dirpath, "lock"), os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW)
        except OSError:
            continue
        try:
            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        except OSError:
            os.close(fd)
            for h in held:
                os.close(h)
            return None
        held.append(fd)
    return held


def idle_cutoff(starts: list[float], quiet_s: float, now: float | None = None) -> float:
    """The local CAS is idle when nothing in it was touched at or after this: the oldest running build's start or
    the start of the quiet window, whichever is earlier."""
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    return min([now - quiet_s, *starts])


def crash_time(report: Path) -> tuple[float, bool] | None:
    """(when it crashed, whether the crashing thread was in Xcode's CAS plugin) for one .ips report, or None when it
    cannot be read. The report's own timestamp, not its mtime: macOS rewrites old reports when it submits them."""
    try:
        header, _, body = report.read_text(errors="replace").partition("\n")
        head, data = json.loads(header), json.loads(body)
        when = datetime.datetime.strptime(head["timestamp"], "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S.%f %z").timestamp()
        thread = data["threads"][data.get("faultingThread", 0)]
        images = data["usedImages"]
        in_plugin = any((images[f["imageIndex"]].get("name") or "") == CAS_PLUGIN
                        for f in thread.get("frames", []) if 0 <= f.get("imageIndex", -1) < len(images))
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, IndexError, TypeError, AttributeError):  # a partly written report
        return None
    return when, in_plugin


def corrupt_local_cas(local: Path, reports: Path | None = None, now: float | None = None) -> str:
    """Why the local CAS is corrupt, "" when nothing says so: an Xcode process crashed inside the CAS plugin after
    the CAS was created. LLVM's on-disk CAS keeps its index in a memory-mapped file, so an unclean shutdown can leave
    the index naming bytes that were never written; from then on every build that inserts there crashes with
    "The Xcode build system has crashed" (cmux7s, 2026-11-10 to 11-17: 29 SWBBuildService crashes in
    OnDiskHashMappedTrie::insertLazy, every trusted seed red), and the size budget never drops it because a build
    that crashes never grows it."""
    reports = CRASH_REPORTS if reports is None else reports
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    try:
        st = local.stat()
    except OSError:
        return ""
    born = getattr(st, "st_birthtime", st.st_ctime)
    newest = None
    for client in CAS_CLIENTS:
        for report in reports.glob(f"{client}*.ips"):
            try:
                if report.stat().st_mtime < max(born, now - CRASH_LOOKBACK):
                    continue
            except OSError:
                continue
            seen = crash_time(report)
            # the report's own time, not its mtime, decides both the lookback and "after the CAS was made"
            fresh = seen is not None and seen[1] and seen[0] > max(born, now - CRASH_LOOKBACK)
            if fresh and (newest is None or seen[0] > newest[0]):
                newest = (seen[0], report.name)
    if newest is None:
        return ""
    return f"corrupt: {newest[1]} crashed in {CAS_PLUGIN} after the local CAS was created"


def drop_local_cas(local: Path, building: str, quiet_s: float, corrupt: bool = False) -> tuple[str, Path | None]:
    """Rename the local CAS aside when no build can be using it (the module docstring): (what happened, the renamed
    path or None). Removing the renamed tree is the caller's, once every lock is released. A corrupt one goes
    without the idle check: any build that touches it crashes, so a running build gains nothing from it, and its
    lock still keeps a process that has it open from losing it mid-use."""

    def not_idle(building: str, quick: bool) -> str:
        """Why the CAS may be in use by a running build, "" when none runs or none has touched it."""
        if not building:
            return ""
        starts = build_starts()
        if starts is None:
            return f"deferred: a build is running ({building}) and its start cannot be read"
        cutoff = idle_cutoff(starts, quiet_s)
        touched = last_touch(local, quick=quick)
        if touched >= cutoff:
            return (f"deferred: a build is running ({building}) and the local CAS was used "
                    f"{max(0.0, time.time() - touched) / 60:.0f} min ago, at or after "
                    f"{time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z', time.localtime(cutoff))}")
        return ""

    if not corrupt and (why := not_idle(building, quick=False)):  # the full walk, with no lock held: the slow part
        return why, None
    cas = cas_locks(local) if local.is_dir() else []
    if cas is None:
        return "deferred: a process has the local CAS open (it holds the CAS lock)", None
    lock = None
    try:
        # While runner jobs run they each hold it shared, so an exclusive hold never comes on a busy mini (cmux9s,
        # 2026-11-15: "deferred: a build holds the host lock" after #1998). Shared still keeps a fleet build
        # (with-host-lock, exclusive) from starting until the rename is done, and fails while one runs.
        lock = take_lock(HOST_LOCK, shared=bool(building))
        if lock is None:
            return "deferred: a build holds the host lock", None
        again = running_commands()
        if again is None:
            return "deferred: a build started (ps failed)", None
        if not corrupt and (why := not_idle(busy(again), quick=True)):
            return why, None
        if not local.is_dir():
            return "removed", None
        renamed = ROOT / f"{PRUNING}{os.getpid()}"
        os.rename(local, renamed)
        return "removed", renamed
    finally:
        if lock not in (None, -1):
            os.close(lock)
        for fd in cas:
            os.close(fd)


def running_commands() -> list[str] | None:
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(["ps", "-axww", "-o", "command="], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    return out.stdout.splitlines() if out.returncode == 0 else None


def is_writer(root: Path) -> bool:
    """The writer's env file names its signing key (glaeda-fleet-cas writer)."""
    try:
        for line in (root / "fleet-cas.env").read_text().splitlines():
            key, _, value = line.partition("=")
            if key.strip() == "FLEET_CAS_SIGN_KEY" and value.strip().strip("'\""):
                return True
    except OSError:
        pass
    return False


def tree_bytes(path: Path) -> int:
    total = 0
    walked(path)
    for dirpath, _, files in os.walk(path, onerror=disk_error):
        for f in files:
            try:
                total += os.lstat(os.path.join(dirpath, f)).st_blocks * 512
            except OSError as e:
                disk_error(e, os.path.join(dirpath, f))
    return total


TMP_STALE_S = 6 * 3600  # a CAS temp file untouched this long belongs to no live write


def node_files(store: Path, subs: tuple[str, ...] = ("cas", "kv")) -> list[tuple[float, int, str]]:
    """(mtime, bytes, path) of every object (cas/) and index entry (kv/) under subs; stats.json and anything else
    stays. A name with a dot is an in-flight write (the daemon writes name.tmp<pid>.<seq> and renames it when done,
    as fleet-cas gc.rs skips them): unlinking one would fail that write and the build, so it is never counted or
    removed while young. One untouched for TMP_STALE_S is a write an interrupted daemon left; it counts and goes
    first."""
    out = []
    stale_before = time.time() - TMP_STALE_S
    walked(store)
    for sub in subs:
        for dirpath, _, files in os.walk(store / sub, onerror=disk_error):
            for f in files:
                p = os.path.join(dirpath, f)
                try:
                    st = os.lstat(p)
                except OSError as e:
                    disk_error(e, p)
                    continue
                if "." in f:
                    if st.st_mtime >= stale_before:
                        continue  # in flight
                    out.append((0.0, st.st_blocks * 512, p))  # abandoned: before every object
                    continue
                out.append((st.st_mtime, st.st_blocks * 512, p))
    return out


def plan_node(files: list[tuple[float, int, str]], budget: int, total: int | None = None) -> list[tuple[float, int, str]]:
    """Oldest first until the store (total, by default the files' sum) fits in 90% of the budget; nothing while it is
    under budget."""
    total = sum(b for _, b, _ in files) if total is None else total
    if total <= budget:
        return []
    goal = int(budget * 0.9)
    doomed = []
    for entry in sorted(files):
        if total <= goal:
            break
        doomed.append(entry)
        total -= entry[1]
    return doomed


def sweep(root: Path) -> int:
    """Delete local CAS copies a previous run renamed aside but did not finish removing."""
    freed = 0
    for leftover in root.glob(PRUNING + "*"):
        freed += tree_bytes(leftover)
        shutil.rmtree(leftover, ignore_errors=True)
    return freed


def take_lock(path: Path, shared: bool = False):
    """The host lock without waiting, or None when a build holds it. No lock file: no lock. shared: runner jobs hold
    it shared for their whole run (docs/CMUX_MINI_RUNNER.md), so only a fleet build's exclusive hold refuses it."""
    try:
        fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW)  # flock needs no write access
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return -1
    try:
        fcntl.flock(fd, (fcntl.LOCK_SH if shared else fcntl.LOCK_EX) | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
    except OSError as e:
        os.close(fd)
        if e.errno in (errno.EWOULDBLOCK, errno.EAGAIN):
            return None
        raise
    return fd


def free_fraction(path: Path) -> float:
    usage = shutil.disk_usage(path)
    return usage.free / usage.total if usage.total else 1.0


def store_gc(root: Path, keep_days: int, now: float | None = None, free: float | None = None) -> dict:
    """Daily `fleet-cas gc` on the store host: dry run, then the real run only when it is safe. Under disk pressure
    (less than GC_PRESSURE of the volume free) it keeps half the days, at least GC_MIN_KEEP_DAYS, every 6 hours."""
    store, binary, stamp = root / "fleet-store", root / "bin" / "fleet-cas", root / "run" / "gc.last"
    if not (store / "kv").is_dir() or not (store / "cas").is_dir():
        return {}
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    free = free_fraction(store) if free is None else free
    pressed = free < GC_PRESSURE
    every = GC_EVERY_PRESSED if pressed else GC_EVERY
    if pressed:
        keep_days = max(GC_MIN_KEEP_DAYS, keep_days // 2)
    try:
        if now - stamp.stat().st_mtime < every:
            return {"gc": "not due"}
    except OSError as e:  # absent: due; unreadable (EIO on the store volume): counted, and gc reports the rest
        disk_error(e, stamp)

    def gc(*extra: str) -> tuple[int, str]:
        try:
            out = subprocess.run([str(binary), "gc", str(store), "--keep-days", str(keep_days), *extra],
                                 capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=3600)
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
            return 1, str(e)
        # The report is the last stdout line; stderr is kept apart so it cannot push it out.
        return out.returncode, (out.stdout.strip()[-400:] + (f" | stderr: {out.stderr.strip()[-200:]}"
                                                            if out.returncode else ""))

    try:
        stamp.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        stamp.touch()  # one attempt a day, whatever the outcome
    except OSError as e:
        disk_error(e, stamp)
    walked(store)
    code, dry = gc("--dry-run")
    if u := GC_UNREADABLE.search(dry):  # entries gc itself skipped as unreadable (gc.rs): the same disk errors
        DISK_ERRORS["count"] += int(u.group(1))
        if int(u.group(1)) and len(DISK_ERRORS["examples"]) < DISK_ERRORS_EXAMPLES:
            DISK_ERRORS["examples"].append(f"{store}: fleet-cas gc skipped {u.group(1)} unreadable entries")
    m = GC_REPORT.search(dry)
    if code != 0 or not m:
        return {"gc": "skipped: the dry run failed or fleet-cas predates gc", "gc_dry_run": dry}
    if int(m.group(1)) != 0:
        return {"gc": "skipped: kept entries name no stored object, so the ID heuristic is suspect",
                "gc_dry_run": dry}
    code, real = gc()
    return {"gc": "ran" if code == 0 else "failed", "gc_keep_days": keep_days, "gc_free": round(free, 3),
            "gc_dry_run": dry, "gc_run": real}


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    ap.add_argument("--apply", action="store_true", help="delete (default: plan only)")
    ap.add_argument("--node-gib", type=float, default=20, help="node-store budget (default %(default)s)")
    ap.add_argument("--local-cas-gib", type=float, default=20,
                    help="the local Xcode CAS goes whole above this (default %(default)s)")
    ap.add_argument("--local-cas-quiet-min", type=float, default=60,
                    help="while builds run, the local CAS goes only if untouched this long (default %(default)s)")
    ap.add_argument("--node-only", action="store_true",
                    help="only the node-store cap: no local CAS, no sweep, no fleet-store gc")
    ap.add_argument("--cap-only", action="store_true",
                    help="only the node-store cap and the local CAS cap: no sweep, no fleet-store gc")
    ap.add_argument("--store-gc-only", action="store_true",
                    help="only the fleet-store gc, for a store host with a custom store root under /Volumes")
    ap.add_argument("--gc-keep-days", type=int, default=14,
                    help="on the store host: fleet-cas gc keeps entries used within this many days")
    a = ap.parse_args(argv)
    if a.cap_only and (a.node_only or a.store_gc_only):
        ap.error("--cap-only excludes --node-only and --store-gc-only")
    caps = a.node_only or a.cap_only  # the caps alone: no sweep, no gc, and the node cap needs no build check
    service = "fleet-cas-store-gc" if a.store_gc_only else "fleet-cas-prune"
    DISK_ERRORS.update(count=0, examples=[], walked=False)
    rec: dict = {"at": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z"), "apply": a.apply}
    if a.store_gc_only:
        # A dedicated store volume (glaeda-fleet-cas checks it is its own APFS volume under /Volumes) holds no mini
        # cache and no toolchain, and the host need not run glaeda-disk, so the mini guards below do not apply.
        if not os.fspath(ROOT).startswith("/Volumes/"):
            rec["result"] = f"refused: --store-gc-only needs a store root under /Volumes, not {ROOT}"
            emit(rec, service)
            return 2
        rec.update(store_gc(ROOT, a.gc_keep_days) if a.apply else {"gc": "plan only"})
        rec["result"] = "ok"
        emit(rec, service)
        return 0
    if a.apply and (held := reserved_above_floor()):  # a reserved host's caches are its holder's until the floor
        rec["result"] = f"deferred: {held}"
        emit(rec, service)
        return 0
    if a.apply and (held := toolchain_overlap([ROOT, ROOT / "cas", ROOT / "node-store" / "cas", ROOT / "node-store" / "kv"])):
        rec["result"] = f"deferred: {held}"
        emit(rec, service)
        return 0
    if a.apply and not caps:
        rec.update(store_gc(ROOT, a.gc_keep_days))
    if not (ROOT / "fleet-cas.env").exists():
        rec["result"] = "no fleet-cas on this host"
        emit(rec, service)
        return 0
    writer = is_writer(ROOT)
    rec["role"] = "writer" if writer else "reader"
    commands = running_commands()
    if commands is None and not caps:
        rec["result"] = "deferred: ps failed, cannot rule out a running build"
        emit(rec, service)
        return 0
    building = busy(commands) if commands is not None else "ps failed"  # the caps: the node cap needs no build check
    if a.apply and not caps:
        rec["swept_bytes"] = sweep(ROOT)
    # Measure and plan with no lock held: these walks are the slow part.
    local = ROOT / "cas"
    # measured in every mode (--node-only too): glaeda-disk's cache budget counts both stores from this report
    local_bytes = tree_bytes(local) if local.is_dir() else 0
    rec["local_cas_bytes"] = local_bytes
    corrupt = corrupt_local_cas(local) if not writer and not a.node_only else ""
    drop_local = not writer and not a.node_only and (local_bytes > a.local_cas_gib * GIB or bool(corrupt))
    ps_failed = drop_local and commands is None  # only --cap-only gets here without ps: the local CAS waits
    store = ROOT / "node-store"
    files = node_files(store) if store.is_dir() else []
    rec["node_store_bytes"] = sum(b for _, b, _ in files)
    # the writer's kv/ entries stay (above); its objects count against the cap with them
    prunable = [f for f in files if not writer or f"{os.sep}kv{os.sep}" not in f[2][len(os.fspath(store)):]]
    doomed = plan_node(prunable, int(a.node_gib * GIB), total=rec["node_store_bytes"])
    rec["local_cas"] = ("not judged (--node-only)" if a.node_only
                        else "kept: the writer empties it before every writer build" if writer
                        else "deferred: ps failed, cannot rule out a running build" if ps_failed
                        else (f"would remove ({corrupt})" if corrupt
                              else "would remove" if drop_local else "within budget"))
    rec["node_store"] = f"would remove {len(doomed)} oldest files, {sum(b for _, b, _ in doomed) / GIB:.1f} GiB"
    if not a.apply:
        rec["result"] = "ok"
        emit(rec, service)
        return 0
    freed = 0
    if doomed:  # the hard cap: no lock and no build check; a file a running build still wants is fetched again
        for _, b, p in doomed:
            try:
                os.unlink(p)
                freed += b
            except OSError as e:  # gone already, or unreadable: counted, and the rest of the cap still goes
                disk_error(e, p)
        rec["node_store"] = f"removed {len(doomed)} oldest files, {freed / GIB:.1f} GiB"
    rec["node_store_freed_bytes"] = freed
    renamed, rec["result"] = None, "ok"
    if ps_failed:
        rec["result"] = rec["local_cas"]
    elif drop_local:
        rec["local_cas"], renamed = drop_local_cas(local, building, a.local_cas_quiet_min * 60, corrupt=bool(corrupt))
        if rec["local_cas"] != "removed":
            rec["result"] = rec["local_cas"]
        elif corrupt:
            rec["local_cas"] = f"removed ({corrupt})"
    if renamed is not None:  # the slow part, with the locks released: nothing reads this path
        shutil.rmtree(renamed, ignore_errors=True)
    emit(rec, service)
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
